@database "example"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: example.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Example plugin"
@{h1}Example plugin

This plugin is a template for plugin authors: a small tool server to copy
when you start a plugin of your own. Its three tools are deliberately
trivial -- a calculator, a text formatter and a status report -- so that
what remains to read is the pattern: how tools are declared and routed,
how settings are read, and the contract every tool handler keeps. It has
no hooks, no panel and stores nothing. It is switched on in the shipped
configuration, but no shipped agent is allowed its tools.

@{code}
For plugin authors
  @{"  What each part shows  " link template}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The calculator  " link calculator}
  @{"  The formatter  " link formatter}
  @{"  The status report  " link status}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node template "What each part shows"
@{h1}What each part shows

Copy the folder, rename it (the folder name is the plugin type), then
replace the tools. The long form of every point is
@{" Writing a plugin " link scarabhive/plugins} and
@{" Tools " link scarabhive/tools}.

@{table}
File | Shows
plugin.toml | the manifest: name, @{tt}type = ["tool-server"]@{utt}, the entrypoint @{tt}plugin:PLUGIN_FACTORY@{utt}, @{tt}requires@{utt}
plugin.py | the factory: the server class itself, called as (name, system_config, server_config)
schema.yaml | tools named @{tt}{{ name }}_calculator@{utt}: the instance name becomes the prefix. The @{tt}config:@{utt} block only documents the settings; the code holds the defaults
server.py | a @{tt}SchemaBasedToolServer@{utt} with one async method per tool, named without the prefix -- @{tt}example_calculator@{utt} runs @{tt}calculator()@{utt}; no @{tt}call()@{utt} of its own
cli.py | an optional command line that drives the handlers without an agent
tests/ | tools called directly and through @{tt}call_with_status@{utt}, the path agents use
@{body}

@{h2}The handler contract

Every handler in @{tt}server.py@{utt} does the four things the framework
does not do for it:

@{bullet}@{b}Checks its own arguments.@{ub} The framework does not validate
arguments against the schema; @{tt}required@{utt} and @{tt}enum@{utt} are
hints to the model. The status tool, for instance, refuses the string
@{tt}"false"@{utt} for @{tt}verbose@{utt} -- it is truthy and would switch
the detail on.
@{bullet}@{b}Answers errors, does not raise them.@{ub}
@{tt}{"status": "error", "error": "..."}@{utt}, the text telling the model
what to change.
@{bullet}@{b}Ends its status line with the result.@{ub} The helper
@{tt}_end()@{utt} calls @{tt}status.end()@{utt} with a line such as
@{tt}add 10, 5 = 15@{utt}; without it the chat shows only "completed". An
error answer needs no line of its own: the framework reports it.
@{bullet}@{b}Keeps its answer bounded.@{ub} The formatter refuses text
longer than @{tt}max_text_length@{utt} instead of echoing any length back.

@{h2}What it does not show

Hooks, a panel, storage (data paths come from @{tt}data_path()@{utt}),
cancellation, and reloading settings without a restart. Plugins that do:
@{" Hooks " link scarabhive/hooks},
@{" Panels and the UI kit " link scarabhive/panels}.
@endnode

@node calculator "The calculator"
@{h1}The calculator

@{tt}example_calculator@{utt} (the prefix follows the instance name)
applies one arithmetic operation to two numbers and rounds the result.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
operation | required; @{tt}add@{utt}, @{tt}subtract@{utt}, @{tt}multiply@{utt} or @{tt}divide@{utt}
a | required; a number, or text that reads as one (@{tt}"5"@{utt}, @{tt}"1e3"@{utt}). Not true/false, not infinite
b | required; as @{tt}a@{utt}
@{body}

The operation runs in decimal arithmetic, then the result is rounded to
@{tt}precision@{utt} decimals (setting, default 2; halves round to even).
A result with more digits than decimal arithmetic holds (28) is returned
unrounded -- at that size a float has no decimals left anyway.

@{h2}The answer

@{table}
Field | Meaning
operation | the operation
operands | a and b, as floats
result | the rounded result, as a float
precision | the decimals it was rounded to
@{body}

@{h2}Errors

@{table}
error | When
Invalid operation '<operation>'. Valid: ['add', 'subtract', 'multiply', 'divide'] | missing or unknown operation
Missing required parameters: a, b | a or b missing
a and b must be numbers, got <a> and <b> | not readable as a number
a and b must be finite numbers within float range | infinite, not a number, or beyond a float
Division by zero is not allowed | divide with b 0
The result of <operation> is too large for a float | the result is beyond a float (1e200 times 1e200)
@{body}

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"operation": "divide", "a": 1, "b": 3}
@{body}

@{code json}
{"operation": "divide", "operands": [1.0, 3.0], "result": 0.33, "precision": 2}
@{body}
@endnode

@node formatter "The formatter"
@{h1}The formatter

@{tt}example_formatter@{utt} applies one transformation to a text.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
text | required; at most @{tt}max_text_length@{utt} characters (setting, default 1000). Longer text is refused, not cut
format | required; @{tt}uppercase@{utt}, @{tt}lowercase@{utt}, @{tt}title@{utt} or @{tt}reverse@{utt}
@{body}

@{tt}title@{utt} is Python's: every letter after a non-letter becomes a
capital, so "it's" becomes "It'S". @{tt}reverse@{utt} reverses the
characters; an emoji built from several characters falls apart.

@{h2}The answer

@{table}
Field | Meaning
original | the text as given
format | the transformation
formatted | the result
length | characters in the result -- can differ from the original ("ß" in uppercase is "SS")
@{body}

@{h2}Errors

@{table}
error | When
text must be a string | text missing or not text
Text length <n> exceeds maximum <max> | text longer than the setting
Invalid format '<format>'. Valid: ['uppercase', 'lowercase', 'title', 'reverse'] | missing or unknown format
@{body}

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"text": "hello world", "format": "title"}
@{body}

@{code json}
{"original": "hello world", "format": "title", "formatted": "Hello World", "length": 11}
@{body}
@endnode

@node status "The status report"
@{h1}The status report

@{tt}example_status@{utt} reports the server's name, how many tools it
has and, on request, its settings.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
verbose | true adds the settings and the tool names; default false. Anything but true or false is an error: "verbose must be true or false, got <value>"
@{body}

@{table}
Field | Meaning
server_name | the instance name
status | always @{tt}active@{utt}
tools_count | 3
version | @{tt}1.0.0@{utt}, fixed in the code
config | with verbose: @{tt}precision@{utt} and @{tt}max_text_length@{utt}
available_tools | with verbose: the three tool names
@{body}
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Tool and parameter descriptions come from @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}; the
three tools together are about 1,230 characters in every request of an
agent allowed them.

@{h2}Errors

Every refusal is an answer with @{tt}status@{utt} @{tt}error@{utt} and the
texts listed under each tool; none raises. The model reads what to
change from the text.

@{h2}The status line

The chat's status line ends with the result:

@{table}
Tool | Line
calculator | @{tt}add 10, 5 = 15@{utt}
formatter | @{tt}reverse: 5 -> 5 chars@{utt}
status | @{tt}example: active, 3 tools@{utt}
@{body}

An error ends the line with the error text.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Answers are ordinary tool results appended to the history; the plugin
injects nothing and changes nothing earlier, so the prompt cache stays
intact. The largest answer is the formatter's: the text twice, at most
about 2,000 characters with the default setting.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} is there and switched on:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    example:
      type: example
      enabled: true
@{body}

No shipped agent allows its tools. To give them to an agent, add to its
list:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+example/*"
@{body}

@{h2}Server settings

Flat keys in the server entry, read once when the server is created;
after a change, restart.

@{table}
Key | Default in code | Meaning
precision | 2 | decimals the calculator rounds to
max_text_length | 1000 | longest text the formatter accepts
@{body}

Both go through @{tt}int()@{utt}: a fraction is cut (2.9 is 2), and a
value that is no number stops the server from being created.

@{h2}Dependencies and data

None beyond the core. The plugin stores nothing.

@{h2}Running it on its own

From the repository root, with @{tt}src@{utt} on the Python path:

@{code text}
python -m plugins.example.cli calculator -o add -a 10 -b 5 [-p 2]
python -m plugins.example.cli formatter -t "hello world" -f uppercase [-m 1000]
python -m plugins.example.cli status [-v] [-p 2] [-m 1000]
@{body}

It builds a server named @{tt}cli@{utt} with the given settings, prints
the answer as JSON and exits with 1 when the answer is an error.
@endnode
